 Record design decisions, hypotheses, review findings, and unresolved questions here.
 Imported associated notes retain original evidence/timestamps in `evidence/associated_notes.jsonl`.
+
+## 2026-10-24 parametric reviewer build
+
+**Decision.** Preserve the exact catalog Racket bytes as the `canonical` setting and add one episode-fixed `preserve_vertical` setting. Step A identifies the count-derived large and small repeated roles, computes their bounding boxes, and derives the boolean intermediate `big_above` from their doubled row centers. Step B builds the canonical packed grid and either keeps it or rotates it 180 degrees so the packed result preserves the source roles' vertical order.
+
+**Teaching design.** The first two demonstrations force opposite `big_above` branches. The sampler supports 2–6 demonstrations and 1–4 independently prefix-stable queries. Query four is a dense 30x30 boundary case rather than padded small content. A finite audit checks the other setting, fixed-below, reversed-branch, and horizontal-proxy rivals in both Python and protected Racket.
+
+**Causal evidence.** The retained skip-A witness uses a below-case. Replacing the `preserve_vertical` program with the canonical program leaves the packed output in the wrong orientation, and protected-Racket reviewer replay confirms the outputs differ.
+
+**Verification and limit.** The focused suite reports 26/26, the staged task suite reports 34/34, native qualification covers both settings on seeds 0 and 17 with 40 protected-Racket evaluations, and the finite self-audit reports zero surviving declared rivals. This is reviewer-ready source, not owner acceptance or training admission. Finite rivals do not prove universal identifiability.
+
+**Portable test repair.** The inherited task test imported an unavailable repository-only path and asserted that two distinct legacy Racket source texts were identical to the current canonical source. It now resolves canonical/base data through `reviewer_support`, keeps the current rule-hash check, and executes both distinct legacy programs through protected Racket against all three canonical pairs. This changes test portability and semantics only; production generator bytes remain unchanged.
